11:28:43 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The user's skepticism makes sense given their experience—negative ground really has dominated modern vehicles. I should acknowledge that while being honest about positive ground's actual historical presence: it was genuinely used in British cars and some European models through the 1960s and into the early 1970s, though it was never as widespread as negative ground became.
11:28:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fair challenge, and your experience tracks with reality—if we're talking about cars from roughly the 1960s onward, negative ground is essentially universal. You're not going to stumble across a positive ground exception in that era unless you're specifically working on something unusual. To be more precise about where positive ground actually existed: - **US manufacturers**: Ford, GM, Chrysler were mostly negative ground by the mid-to-late 1950s. Ford switched around 1956. So if you're talking about any mainstream American car from the last 65+ years, negative ground is what you'd expect 100% of the time. - **British cars**: This is really where positive ground hung on longest—brands like Austin, Morris, Triumph, and MG used positive ground into the 1960s and in some cases very early 1970s before they too switched over. - **Older/pre-war vehicles**: Positive ground was more mixed/common in general before standardization took hold. So realistically, unless you've worked on a vintage British car or something from the 1950s or earlier, you'd have zero chance of encountering positive ground in your dozen cars. It's not that I was implying it's some common thing you'd trip over doing regular repair work today—it's a historical footnote at this point, essentially extinct in anything you'd find in a driveway or shop now. Your pushback is legitimate: for practical purposes in the modern era (and really the last six decades), negative ground is the standard, full stop. I probably should've been clearer that positive ground is a "used to exist, now essentially gone" thing rather than framing it as if it's still floating around as some parallel option.
